Version: 1.0
desc: Break Room (mini games) by chmaha
tags: game games puzzle fun break
author: chmaha
changelog:
  Initial release.
about:
  Little games to play when you need a breather, in two groups on the home screen: Zen and Games.
  Koi Pond: a calm pond with koi of eight varieties, lily pads, lotus flowers and falling blossom. Left-click
  to drop food and the koi come to eat; right-click or right-drag through the water to make ripples. Choose
  how many koi swim (1 to 12) and switch between day and night. There is nothing to win or lose.
  Patch Bay: a tangle of glowing, sagging patch cables. Drag the jacks around until no two cables cross.
  Level 1 starts with one crossing, level 2 with two, and so on, with more jacks as you go.
  Slingshot: press anywhere on the board, pull back and let go to launch a comet from the launch ring. The
  planets' gravity bends its path. Get inside the portal's swirling ring and it pulls you in to clear the level;
  grab the three stars on the way for a perfect shot. Hitting a planet, leaving the board or getting stuck in
  orbit costs you the shot. The aim guide and the portal's pull get smaller as you go, and later levels add
  pulsars that push away.
  Pixel Garden: a floating pixel-art island. Pick Flower, Tree or Mushroom and click a tile to plant a random
  variety; it grows from seed to bloom in real time and keeps growing while the window is closed. Decor places
  a bench, fountain, stone lantern, little pond, birdbath, rocks, a garden gnome or a bird feeder that robins,
  blue tits, goldfinches, cardinals and sparrows visit (and an owl at night). Water a plant to speed it up for
  a few minutes (rain showers now and then water everything), dig anything up with the shovel, press Grow all
  to see the finished garden, and switch the sky between day, sunset and night. Nothing ever wilts.
  Rain Window: rain running down a misted window, with an out-of-focus city at night, a city at dusk or a
  forest behind it. Big drops gather the small ones and slide down in fits and starts, leaving clear trails.
  Drag to wipe the mist (or draw in it); it slowly creeps back. Choose light, steady or heavy rain.
  Kintsugi: a broken piece of pottery waits on the table. Drag each shard to where it belongs; close is close
  enough, it settles into place and the crack fills with gold. Five glazes to mend, with more shards as you go.
  Firefly Shepherd: a night meadow full of fireflies that drift and flock together, each blinking in its own
  time. Every night has its own sky: from dusk to deep night, the moon waxes and wanes, shooting stars cross
  now and then, and dark nights may bring the northern lights or the Milky Way. Your light leads them; hold the mouse button to call them closer. Guide them into the paper lanterns,
  and when every lantern glows they float off into the sky. More lanterns each night.
  Signal Path: a grid of cable tiles, pedals and splitters between an audio input and a room full of
  speakers. Click a tile to turn it clockwise, right-click to turn it back. The signal lights up everything
  connected to the input; route it to every speaker with no loose ends to clear the level. Beat par for the
  layout for a perfect routing. The grid grows as you go.
  Peggy: aim the launcher and click to drop a ball into a field of pegs over a painted landscape.
  Pegs light up when the ball hits them and clear once it leaves the board. Clear every red peg before your
  ten balls run out; long bounce chains score more, gold pegs split the ball in two, and the catcher sliding
  along the bottom gives the ball back. Every red you clear heats the gauge on the right, multiplying your
  points by 2, 3, 5 and finally 10. The last red peg gets the slow-motion treatment, then golden rain: every
  peg left turns gold and is worth ten times as much for the rest of that shot, and the catcher gives way to
  five buckets worth 0, 2500, 5000, 10000 and a 25000 jackpot. After level 10 the boards keep
  changing: pegs that spin and slide, red pegs tucked behind others, teal bumpers that kick the ball away, a
  restless catcher, and a showpiece level built around one giant shape every five levels. Aim with the mouse,
  or with the left and right arrow keys (hold Shift for fine aim) and drop the ball with the down arrow or Enter.
  Mute Point: a mixing desk with a grid of glowing red mute buttons. Clicking a button flips it and the
  buttons directly above, below and beside it. Unmute every button to get all channels live; match par for a
  perfect mix. Hint lights up a button worth pressing. The desk grows from three channels to eight as you go.
  Bubble and Squeak: field mice are trapped in bubbles hanging from a branch on a moonlit night. Aim the
  launcher with the mouse and click to shoot; bubbles bounce off the side twigs. Three or more of a colour
  pop, and anything left hanging falls, so knocking loose a big cluster scores the most. Free every mouse
  before your shots run out: each one floats down on a dandelion seed to the mushroom house. Right-click or
  click the acorn to swap the next bubble in. Or use the keyboard: left and right arrows aim (hold Shift for
  fine aim), the up arrow shoots and the down arrow swaps. Popping fills the power jar, which loads a bomb, a rainbow
  bubble (matches any colour) or a fireball (burns straight through). Later levels add bomb and lightning
  bubbles in the branch, stones that never pop, and a branch that sinks a row every few shots.
  Krypton Gigablast: a vertical shooter through five alien worlds, two levels each. Steer through living walls
  and barriers (they block you and can crush you against the bottom of the screen, but never hurt on touch),
  swarms of creatures, collect the credits they drop and spend them at the shop halfway through and at the
  end of every level: a stronger main gun, side and rear shots, homing missiles, a laser, drones, energy, extra
  ships and smart bombs, or sell what you have for half the price. Every level ends with a guardian or a boss,
  and the Hive Mind waits at the end of world five. Mouse: steer, hold the left button to fire, right-click for
  a smart bomb. Keyboard: arrows steer, hold S to fire, D detonates a smart bomb. Each level, each return from
  the shop and each new ship starts with a 3-2-1 countdown before you take control.
  Flop House: no-limit Texas Hold'em against twelve rogues, from Lucky Lou in the back room to Mr Grim in the
  vault. Every table is a sit-and-go: blinds keep rising and the last player with chips wins it. Each character
  has a real playing style and a tell you can spot (a whistle, a wink, a sweat, a glowing antenna), and knocking
  one out adds it to your Rogues' gallery. Read tokens show how strong a hand really is, the optional Coach gives
  your win chance and pot odds, and Hands shows the rankings and your collection. All-ins get win percentages and a
  slow river. Cards are shuffled fairly and the opponents never see your hand. Mouse only.
  Hold Your Horses: the whole British racing year, Flat and National Hunt, on real courses with their real
  direction of running and typical circuit lengths. Career: buy horses at the monthly sales, train them, enter them
  in any race on the calendar (the 2:50 at Doncaster, the Derby, the Grand National) and pick jockey and tactics or
  ride yourself. Going and weather change every day, meetings can be abandoned, and over jumps horses sometimes
  fall. Or let the game pick a random day or a one-off race and bet with a fresh bank, or follow today's real
  fixtures from the 2026 and 2027 lists. Close finishes go to a photo. Mouse only.
  Hop to It: hop as far as you can across roads, railways and rivers without getting squashed. Ten worlds, each
  with its own hazard, and the seasons change as you travel. All ten worlds and eleven hoppers are open from the
  start, and 40 coins in a world unlock its bonus hopper. Stand still too long or fall behind and the eagle
  swoops. Two players can share a keyboard, split screen or battling on one screen. Arrow keys or W A S D hop,
  or click the way you want to go.
  Press P in any game to pause it (the music stops too) and P again, or Resume, to carry on.
  The pause panel also has the help card, the Sound button and Reset this game, which returns just that game
  to how it was the first time you opened it.
  The Games button goes back to the home screen. Your progress is saved with the project, and Start over
  takes you back to level 1. Koi Pond and Pixel Garden remember their settings, and the garden itself.
  Audio passes through untouched. Game sound is on by default and one Sound button, on the home screen and
  in each game, cycles All, FX only and Off for the whole plugin. It only plays while the transport is stopped or paused,
  so it never ends up in a render or recording.
  Original design by chmaha
  JSFX code released under GPLv3 license
  Claude was used in the creation of this product
  AI-assisted not vibe-coded
  Hold Your Horses course shapes (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright. The course
  geometry data block in this file is made available under the Open Database License (ODbL).
  The music (all BreakRoom-*.opus files) comes from Pixabay under the
  Pixabay Content License and is not covered by the GPL; see the -LICENSE.txt files next to them.
